WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.800
Yes, this new AI has listened to more than&nbsp;
5,000 songs, and what did it learn? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:11.520
it learned to perform text to music similarly to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.520
Google’s MusicLM. But this technique is&nbsp;
truly something else. This is MusicGen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.400
an AI where we enter the kind of music we&nbsp;
wish to hear, and it just gives it to us.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.420
Like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:38.340
Wow. Yes, all this was generated&nbsp;
by an AI. This was a pop dance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.340
track with catchy melodies, tropical percussion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:47.520
and upbeat rhythms with the new MusicGen.&nbsp;
And now let’s compare it to Google’s MusicLM.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:59.220
This one is real good too, and it was&nbsp;
published in this year’s January. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:05.220
the first one you’ve heard, MusicGen&nbsp;
has something up the sleeve. It is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:09.360
open source technique that&nbsp;
is free for everyone. Wow.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:17.400
Now, smooth jazz, with a saxophone solo, piano&nbsp;
chords, and snare drums. First with MusicGen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:29.580
That was real good, with a bit of a&nbsp;
saxophone solo. Perhaps a snippet. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.200
the previous work, MusicLM.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:47.400
I have to say that I really liked the smooth&nbsp;
jazz part better here, that was real good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.540
however, no saxophone. It is one thing to do well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.800
but it is not enough - it also&nbsp;
has to follow the prompt as well.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:03.600
Now, wait a minute. These samples are 30-seconds&nbsp;
long, but real songs are typically much longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:11.340
than that. So is that it? No real songs for us?&nbsp;
Well, don’t despair because this paper has two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:19.140
more absolutely incredible features. One, long&nbsp;
music generation. Oh yes, these are two-minute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:25.620
long samples, but we are experienced Fellow&nbsp;
Scholars here, so we are looking for long-term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:30.480
coherence. That means that we will look at&nbsp;
snippets of different parts of the song,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:37.620
and listen if they really sound like they belong&nbsp;
to the same song. Note that the previous MusicLM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:43.620
was not as good in this task. Here is the&nbsp;
snippet we looked at in a previous episode.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.340 --> 00:03:00.120
Now, let’s see if the new MusicGen is any&nbsp;
better on this. I can’t wait - let’s do it!

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:22.020
Wow. First one is flying colors. Not&nbsp;
even a little bit of a drift. Second one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:54.300
Maybe you noticed that I liked this one&nbsp;
so much that I was a little reluctant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:59.100
to skip forward because I wanted to&nbsp;
just keep listening. Coherence was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:03.660
not as tight as with the previous&nbsp;
one, art and music is subjective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:10.560
but if we have a song that has a little more&nbsp;
variation, this is a good one too. Loving it.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:39.240
And third. This absolutely knocked it out&nbsp;
of the park. Absolutely the same song for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:45.360
two minutes. This is super difficult to pull&nbsp;
off, and in my opinion, the previous MusicLM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:52.440
was not as good as this new technique. So much&nbsp;
improvement in just a few months! Incredible.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:59.760
But we haven’t looked at my absolute favorite&nbsp;
feature yet, I am pretty sure it will be yours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:06.060
too. They call this feature conditioning, this&nbsp;
essentially can take an already existing piece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:12.420
of music and remix it with a text prompt.&nbsp;
Listen to the input. And the output.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:30.960
Wow, this is insanity.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:34.020
However, not even this work is perfect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:38.040
here is something that you might&nbsp;
call somewhat of a failure case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:57.300
Yes, the input was maybe recognizable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:02.760
but it was jumbled up and not&nbsp;
nearly as good as the other samples.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:08.400
But wait, here comes the best part.&nbsp;
All this is available to all of you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:14.520
free of charge. The source code of this paper is&nbsp;
available, and as of the making of this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:20.160
you can even run it on the web, I’ve put&nbsp;
a link to it in the video description. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:26.040
in the future, we will all have our own&nbsp;
AIs that create a nearly infinite amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:32.220
of music personalized to you in real&nbsp;
time. And just imagine what we will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:37.140
be able to do just a couple more papers&nbsp;
down the line. What a time to be alive!

